// Demonstration of the `automaton` package — finite-state machines as a thin layer of
// conventions over the diagram layer.
//
// Render with:  texish scripts/automaton-demo.script

\use{automaton}

\def headline {\italic{The automaton package} \hfil page \the\pageno}
\def footline {\hfil — \the\pageno — \hfil}
\set raggedbottom {1}
\def centerline c {\hbox to:\hsize {\hfil\c\hfil}}

\font lmroman 12 regular

\bold{Finite-state machines}

A state is a circle, an accepting state a double circle, and the start state carries an
incoming arrow. A transition is a labelled arrow; a self-transition is a loop above the
state. The machine below accepts strings over $\{a, b\}$ that contain the substring
$ab$: stay in $q_0$ until an $a$ is read, wait in $q_1$ for the $b$, and once in the
accepting state $q_2$ stay there.

\vskip 12pt

\centerline{\picture width:5in height:1.7in {
  \state{q0}{at 0.7in 0.7in}{$q_0$}
  \state{q1}{right q0 78}{$q_1$}
  \accepting{q2}{right q1 78}{$q_2$}
  \initial{q0}
  \trans[$a$]{q0}{q1}
  \trans[$b$]{q1}{q2}
  \loopabove{q0}{$b$}
  \loopabove{q1}{$a$}
  \loopabove{q2}{$a, b$}
}}

\vskip 16pt

\bold{A cyclic automaton}

States need not lie in a row. Placed at the corners of a triangle, three states count the
number of $1$s read modulo $3$; each $1$ steps to the next state, and $r_0$ — both the
start and the only accepting state — is reached again exactly when the count is divisible
by three. The transitions are curved arcs bowing around the cycle.

\vskip 8pt

\centerline{\picture width:3.2in height:2.3in {
  \accepting{r0}{at 1.5in 1.9in}{$r_0$}
  \state{r1}{at 0.6in 0.5in}{$r_1$}
  \state{r2}{at 2.4in 0.5in}{$r_2$}
  \initial{r0}
  \arc[-30]{r0}{r1}{$1$}
  \arc[-30]{r1}{r2}{$1$}
  \arc[-30]{r2}{r0}{$1$}
}}
